<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Public web traffic stays HTTPS/TLS-only: cleartext is never silently allowed for a
  normal website. Two deliberate, user-facing exceptions exist because a phone
  browser has to open the addresses people really use:

  * loopback and private-LAN destinations (LAN transfer servers, routers, NAS boxes)
    may use HTTP. PhormiNetworkPolicy decides which hosts qualify and the address
    bar shows a "Not secure" state for them.
  * a public host the user chose to open over HTTP after a warning is remembered in
    `cleartext_trusted_hosts`, so a revisit is not blocked by the OS before the
    WebView can show the page.

  Why this file - and not the manifest flag - is the switch: from Android 7.0 (API 24)
  a network security config takes precedence over `android:usesCleartextTraffic`, and
  minSdk here is 26, so the manifest attribute is inert documentation. It stays
  "false" so nothing reads as approval, and the audit keeps it that way. The real
  gate is PhormiNetworkPolicy: it is what decides whether an address loads, what the
  address bar says, and when a warning is shown. The OS has to permit the socket to
  exist, because the WebView must render the page after the user taps Continue.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
